+# Rejected at Step 0: arXiv 2003.05015
+
+Paper: Timothy Duff, Kathlén Kohn, Anton Leykin, and Tomáš Pajdla,
+[*PL1P — Point-line Minimal Problems under Partial Visibility in Three
+Views*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.05015).
+
+## Decision
+
+The prior-triage reconstruction proposal clears **G** and **V** in isolation:
+sample rational calibrated cameras and a rational incident point-line scene,
+project the visible features, and retain the sampled cameras and scene as an
+exact witness.  Exact incidence, `SO(3)`, and projection equations can all be
+checked by rational arithmetic without reading the planted answer.
+
+It does not clear **H on either track**, and the paper supplies no native
+scaling regime that repairs this:
+
+- **Track A fails.**  The paper proves algebraic finiteness and reports
+  algebraic degrees, not computational or distributional hardness.  Its
+  reduced three-view problems have a fixed 11-dimensional camera budget and
+  form a finite catalog.  No result covers the inverse-generated rational
+  distribution proposed in triage.
+- **Track B fails.**  On generic projected data the public route is the same
+  polynomial elimination/numerical-homotopy computation as the mechanical
+  route; the privately sampled scene is not a public shortcut.  The paper's
+  only unbounded construction adds features that Theorem 2 explicitly calls
+  reducible and degree-preserving.  Finding and discarding those features is
+  the same local scan for both the mechanical and purported compact routes.
+
+Implementation therefore stops before Step 1, as required after a Step-0
+failure.  No generator, gate report, README, or oracle transcript has been
+fabricated.
+
+## What the paper actually defines
+
+Section 3 defines a point-line problem by:
+
+1. a realizable, complete incidence relation between projective 3D points and
+   lines;
+2. three calibrated cameras `[R | t]`, with `R in SO(3)` and the gauge fixed by
+   `P1 = [I | 0]` and the first coordinate of `t2` equal to one; and
+3. a visibility pattern saying exactly which globally labelled points and
+   lines each view observes.
+
+Thus feature correspondences are part of the instance.  Hiding them and asking
+for a matching would create a new CSP, not the paper's problem.
+
+A problem is *minimal* when the joint camera map is dominant and a generic
+image has a finite nonempty fiber.  It is *camera-minimal* when only the set of
+camera configurations in that fiber must be finite.  Definition 4 restricts
+the paper to `PL1P`: every 3D line is incident to at most one listed point.
+
+Lemma 2 says minimality is exactly balance plus dominance.  For a reduced
+three-view problem, Section 5 turns balance into Equation (4): a nonnegative
+27-variable weighted sum equal to **11**, the dimension of the camera
+configuration space.  Consequently there are only **845,161** balanced
+signatures, **143,494** classes after relabelling views, and **140,616** reduced
+minimal classes.  Theorem 3 also bounds the only high-valency local feature to
+one point with at most seven pins.  There is no parameter tending to infinity
+inside a reduced camera-solving core.
+
+## Step-0 certificate question
+
+| Candidate native task | Certificate producer | Disposition |
+|---|---|---|
+| Recover cameras and the 3D scene from a generated joint image | Inverse generation: sample the rational cameras/scene first and project them | G and V pass; Track A has no distributional theorem, and generic recovery has no compact public route for Track B |
+| Decide that a balanced signature is minimal | Section 7 samples a finite-field evaluation and computes the full Jacobian rank | A polynomial-time exact linear-algebra certificate producer; also only a finite 143,494-class catalog |
+| Output any minimal signature | Enumerate Equation (4), or look it up in the released classification | Structure-aware guessing is fatal: `140616 / 143494 = 0.9799434...` of the relabelling classes are minimal |
+| Recover the unique reduced core of a padded problem | The four local forgetting rules in Theorem 2 | The theorem itself gives a linear scan; every added feature is superfluous and the degree is unchanged |
+| Use a five-point extension/camera-registration case | Solve the fixed two-view five-point core and then the fixed-size registration problem described in Section 8 and SM Section 11 | A composition of fixed algebraic problems, not an `n`-scaling family |
+| Hide cross-view correspondences or select an inlier subset | No such object occurs in Definition 1: global point/line labels already specify correspondence | This would be a convenience graph/CSP problem and cannot count as native coverage |
+
+The first row is the strongest reading of the prior triage.  Restricting the
+answer language to rational solutions would make its planted witness writable
+and exactly checkable, but it would not make the public inverse map hard on the
+generated distribution.
+
+## Mechanical cost and compact route
+
+Two distinct computations in the paper must not be conflated.
+
+### Classification/minimality
+
+Section 7 tests dominance by evaluating the joint-map Jacobian over a large
+finite field and computing its rank.  I also inspected the authors' released
+`PL1P` data and code.  All 143,494 stored candidate records contain an elapsed
+rank-check time.  Their matrices have dimensions from 24 to 88 (median 53),
+and the recorded rank checks have:
+
+| quantity | measured value |
+|---|---:|
+| median wall time per signature | **6.685969019 s** |
+| 95th percentile | **15.905794536 s** |
+| sum of recorded serial times | **1,185,747.193 s = 13.724 days** |
+
+The repository README independently describes candidate generation as taking
+a few hours and the rank-check run as taking several days.  These are costs of
+building the finite classification, not evidence that a generated solver
+instance is hard.  Once the table exists, the mechanical cost for a supplied
+signature is one table lookup.  Without the table, the compact route is the
+same finite-field rank computation: **one rank computation versus one rank
+computation (ratio 1:1)**.  The paper provides no short invariant that replaces
+it for arbitrary signatures.
+
+### Reconstruction
+
+Section 8 uses an eliminated camera formulation and numerical monodromy/
+homotopy continuation.  Its reported low-degree cases have **64–288** camera
+solutions, and the paper notes previously known cases above **1,000**.  A
+standard solver therefore handles a fixed polynomial support and tracks or
+extracts a fixed number of algebraic branches determined by the chosen
+signature.  Increasing coordinate height raises exact bit cost, but does not
+create a new geometric search parameter; it mainly makes the same arithmetic
+harder to perform by hand.
+
+For generic inverse-generated images, the public compact route has exactly the
+same count as the mechanical one: **one fixed-core elimination/homotopy solve
+versus one fixed-core elimination/homotopy solve (ratio 1:1)**.  Merely knowing
+that the generator sampled one of the solutions does not identify which
+solution it sampled from the rendered image.
+
+For an artificially padded problem with `n` local feature records, Theorem 2
+gives the strongest possible preprocessing.  In an arbitrary public ordering,
+the mechanical route performs `n` local visibility/incidence inspections and
+the compact route must perform the same `n` inspections before invoking the
+unchanged core solver: **`n` versus `n` (again ratio 1:1)**.  At a concrete
+hypothetical `n = 4,096`, both routes require 4,096 record inspections, already
+above the 300-operation no-tool cap.  Making the padding periodic or explicitly
+formulaic would shorten both routes and expose the core to the obvious
+visibility-pattern attack; it would not create a fourth failing Track-B
+in-context attack.
+
+These numbers answer the required Track-B question.  The fact that the paper's
+full catalog took days is not itself a compression gap for one posed instance,
+and the only public shortcuts reduce to the same work as the standard method.
+
+## Why the infinite class does not provide G7
+
+Theorem 1 says a reduction preserves minimality and degree.  Theorem 2 says
+every minimal three-view `PL1P` reduces uniquely and lists exactly four kinds
+of forgotten local feature; applying their inverses generates all the apparent
+unboundedness.  The proof explains that the added structures lift over fixed
+cameras and contribute no new camera constraint.
+
+Therefore increasing `n` has only three possible effects:
+
+1. ask for the full 3D scene, making the answer longer until the 256-atom cap is
+   hit;
+2. ask only for cameras, leaving the algebraic search equal to the same fixed
+   reduced core; or
+3. force a longer scan through data which the paper proves can be discarded.
+
+None grows the haystack around a fixed native witness in the required sense.
+The first grows the needle, the second does not grow difficulty, and the third
+has no mechanical/compact gap.
+
+## Easy regimes and tempting non-solutions checked
+
+- Theorem 2's local reductions are an explicit easy preprocessing regime.
+- Theorem 6 replaces dangling-pin observations while preserving
+  camera-minimality and camera-degree; these are again transformations of a
+  fixed camera solve, not harder cores.
+- Corollary 1's swaps and the six camera relabellings preserve degree and yield
+  only finite equivalences.  They must collapse under `canonical_key`, so they
+  cannot manufacture diversity.
+- Section 8 says that some three-view problems contain the classical five-point
+  problem and decompose into a two-view relative-pose solve followed by a
+  fixed-size camera-registration problem (including P3P).  This is a useful
+  solver construction and exactly why those cases do not supply asymptotic H.
+- The finite-field Jacobian representation is licensed by Section 7, but
+  replacing the geometric task with an arbitrary planted kernel-vector matrix
+  would compile away the point-line incidence geometry.  It would be a linear-
+  algebra analogue, not this paper's native reconstruction problem.
+- Larger random rational coefficients can make manual arithmetic unpleasant,
+  but absent a paper-supported invariant they do not shorten the intended
+  route.  That would test calculation, and eventually answer transcription,
+  rather than geometric insight.
+
+## Gate diagnosis
+
+| requirement | result | evidence |
+|---|---:|---|
+| G — certificate known without solving | Passes in isolation | Sample rational cameras/scene first, then project |
+| V — exact witness verification | Passes in isolation | Exact rational rotation, incidence, and projection identities |
+| H — Track A | **Fails** | No hardness theorem or parameter regime for the planted distribution; reduced cores form a fixed finite catalog |
+| H — Track B | **Fails** | Generic data have no compact route; reduction padding costs `n` inspections mechanically and compactly |
+| G4 for the signature alternative | **Fails** | About 97.994% of relabelling classes are minimal |
+| G7 scaling for reconstruction | **Fails** | Equation (4) fixes every reduced core; inverse reductions preserve degree |
+| Overall | **Rejected at Step 0** | No paper-supported family satisfies G, H, and V simultaneously |
+
+This is not a rejection merely because an algorithm exists.  The mechanical
+and compact costs are recorded above, including the tempting several-day
+classification computation: at the level of a single posed instance the two
+routes coincide, while table lookup makes the classified-signature variant
+even easier.

